      "duties": {
        "work_root": {
          "owner": "member_002",
          "recipient": "member_001",
          "status": "handed_off",
          "scope": "independent diagnosis and evidence handoff",
          "message_sequence": 67,
          "evidence": 65,
          "sender_decisions": [
            {
              "choice": "DIAG",
              "sequence": 67,
              "body": "DIAG 65 Diagnosed cause of the repeated failure of tests/test_ext_autodoc.py::test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables (still failing at v4 opp56 and v7 opp67 after your two replace_file repairs).\n\nFinding (from repo search, event 65): that test is NOT about properties and NOT about the :type: field you are changing. At tests/test_ext_autodoc.py line 1839 it is decorated @pytest.mark.sphinx('html', testroot='ext-autodoc') and calls do_autodoc(app, 'class', 'ta...') with options {\"members\": None, \"undoc-members\": None, \"inherited-members\": None}. It asserts the INSTANCE-variable/attribute output of a class with inherited annotated instance attributes. So it exercises AttributeDocumenter / InstanceAttributeDocumenter output, not PropertyDocumenter.\n\nImplication: your change to PropertyDocumenter.add_directive_header does not affect this test's actual content, so the failure means your edits changed something the attribute path also renders, or (more likely) the expected-output list in that test was edited/mismatched rather than the property path being fixed. Two replace_file attempts followed by the same nodeid failing is the signature of repairing test expectations instead of the source behavior.\n\nRecommended next step (concrete): (1) revert any edits you made inside test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables' expected output; keep that test byte-identical to baseline. (2) Apply the property fix only where properties are rendered: in PropertyDocumenter.add_directive_header replace `objrepr = stringify_typehint(signature.return_annotation)` with restify-based markup for the ':type:' line (restify is already imported at line 36; there is no stringify_annotation in this tree), keeping the existing TypeError/ValueError guard. (3) Add NEW coverage for the issue instead: a @property returning a class type (e.g. '-> Point') asserting the emitted ':type:' contains ':class:`' role markup. (4) Verify with the property/typehints subset plus test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables restored to baseline, not by rewriting its expectations.\n\nI have not modified any files; my workspace is clean."
            }
          ],
          "recipient_disposition": {
            "choice": "ADOPT",
            "reason": "Confirmed and acting on your diagnosis. You are right: test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables exercises Attribute/InstanceAttribute output with inherited-members, not PropertyDocumenter. My error was fixture placement: I put prop1/prop2 on `Derived`, so they leak into that test's inherited-members output (14 extra items). Source fix itself is already correct and evidence-backed: PropertyDocumenter.add_directive_header uses restify(...) for the ':type:' line with the TypeError/ValueError guard, and test_autodoc_typed_instance_variables now PASSES (report test-report-68bbc2e2f37976f6729fe6e1, v7, 1 passed / 1 failed).\n\nRepair plan (keeps your point 1 and 2): move the fixture properties from `Derived` back onto `Class`, and annotate prop1 as `-> Class` (a name already bound when the class body executes) so no forward reference/NameError occurs and nothing leaks into the inherited-members test. Expected ':type: :class:`target.typed_vars.Class`'. test_autodoc_typed_inherited_instance_variables stays byte-identical to baseline. Will re-run the two tests plus the property/typehints subset, then publish a fixed candidate for your open_patch_review -> run_patch_review_tests -> org_review_patch.",
            "message_sequence": 67,
            "response_sequence": 68,
            "own_evidence": null,
